    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">The register is not a window</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The catalogue was a colonial state's instrument for watching what its subjects printed. Its categories, its glosses, its one-line descriptions of contents and the Reporter on Books who wrote them are not noise on the way to the real data — they are the apparatus, and the apparatus is one of the objects of study. So the project models the observer as well as the observed.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">That commitment is enforced by the data model rather than asserted in a preface. The record exists in three layers that are never collapsed: <strong class="text-gray-300">page image → the catalogue's own words, verbatim → a normalised analytic layer.</strong> The registrar's spellings, misprints and editorialising survive in a form that stays queryable. Every normalisation is a numbered decision with its reasoning recorded. Uncertain readings are not guessed; they go to a per-quarter adjudication queue with a stated argument for each. What the concept cannot absorb is kept where it can still be consulted.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">The catalogue validates itself</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Registration numbers run as one dense sequence per year. Serial numbers chain across quarters within each language–subject section. Both are used as independent instruments, so an extraction error surfaces as a break in a sequence rather than passing silently into the dataset — and a broken sequence is either a misread to fix or a genuine anomaly in the register to flag.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A second, independent checker built from the register's own grammar and vocabulary of press names flags roughly five per cent of entries and carries about sevenfold lift on key-field errors. It overlaps the extractor's own uncertainty flags on only 71 entries in the whole corpus, so the two together see substantially more than either alone. Model choice was settled by a scored comparison against a hand-transcribed golden quarter rather than by preference — and is currently being re-opened, because a cheaper model turns out to make fewer key-field errors on the same benchmark.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">An integrity sweep asked whether the stored record matches its own specification, and found that it did not in one consequential way: the copies column stores thousands separators as text, so the most obvious query a user would write undercounts the corpus by 16.7 per cent. It is documented in the open, in the repository, with the correct query beside it. A dataset is trustworthy in proportion to how well it publishes its own defects.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">What is still open</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-12">
      <li><strong class="text-gray-300">Native-script titles.</strong> The catalogue prints every title twice — once in Perso-Arabic, Devanagari or Gurmukhi, once in the registrar's romanisation. Only the romanisation is currently extracted, which means the analysable record is the one the colonial state produced for itself.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-gray-300">Image provenance.</strong> The text of the catalogue is a public-domain government record. The terms under which the page scans were supplied are an open question, and are being answered rather than assumed.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-gray-300">The people in it.</strong> This dataset names authors, publishers and printers from a print ecology much of which ended up on the wrong side of a partition, and whose descendants exist. The commitment is to publish the apparatus alongside the data, so the pipeline is as criticisable as the registrar was.</li>
    </ul>

